# Code owners — GitHub requests a review from the owner when a matching path
# changes. Order matters: the LAST matching pattern wins for a given file.
# See https://docs.github.com/articles/about-code-owners.

# Default owner for everything not matched more specifically below.
*                        @joshcoyne

# The engine. `crates/` is the Rust half and `packages/` the TypeScript half,
# laid out by tier — see docs/architecture.md.
/crates/                 @joshcoyne
/packages/               @joshcoyne

# The boundary between the two halves is the thing a change can break
# invisibly, so guard both sides of it with their own lines: krudd-web's
# exported surface and the wrapper over it are one contract in two files, and
# they are hand-written until #817 generates them from one spec.
/crates/shell/web/       @joshcoyne
/packages/base/boundary/ @joshcoyne

# Build and CI/release machinery. `cargo xtask` is the one build entry point;
# there is no build step outside it.
/xtask/                  @joshcoyne
/Cargo.toml              @joshcoyne
/rust-toolchain.toml     @joshcoyne
/.cargo/                 @joshcoyne
/.github/                @joshcoyne

# Version source — maintained by release-please, but guard it so a manual edit
# still routes a review. version.txt is what xtask stamps into the build; the
# manifest and config drive release-please itself.
/version.txt                    @joshcoyne
/.release-please-manifest.json  @joshcoyne
/release-please-config.json     @joshcoyne
